\par\par \b \uc2  China may join Asia devaluation club, analysts say. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 1997\par 04:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 3 (Reuters) - China, fighting a lonely battle to check the rise of the yuan, may eventually join the regional club of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations in a bid to maintain its export momentum, senior Japan-based analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But any devaluation of the yuan would be mild, given Beijing's policy of emphasising stability in both the political and economic arenas, they said. \par \par \uc2  "China is likely to allow the yuan to fall gradually towards 8.7 (to the dollar) in the medium-term to keep its competitive edge over regional trade rivals," said Mitsuru Toida, chief economist at the Institute of Developing Economies (IDE), a research affiliate of Japan's Trade Ministry. \par \par \uc2  While other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunge, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the dollar for the yuan in an effort to check rises in the yuan resulting from a large trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  The yuan was at 8.2799 to the dollar on Wednesday -- close to a 30-month high. \par \par \uc2  Toida said China's stress on stability since the death of paramount leader Deng Xiaoping in February would prevent any drastic depreciation of the yuan. \par \par \uc2  "Instead of a one-off devaluation, China may pursue a crawling peg policy and allow the yuan to depreciate at a gradual and steady pace," said C.H. Kwan, senior economist at Nomura Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  China's commitment to stability would persist as long as it enjoyed large current account surpluses and massive foreign reserves, Toida said. \par \par \uc2  China had $132.9 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the end of August -- more than any other nation except Japan. \par \par \uc2  "China appears interested in holding massive foreign reserves to secure its voice on regional matters and on global political and economic issues," Toida said. \par \par \uc2  If Beijing decided to devalue the yuan by a large margin, it could find itself having to spend much more of its precious foreign reserves to counter speculative attacks, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  China has relaxed some of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls and allowed Chinese exporters to hold on to some of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  from November in order to take some upward presssure off the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the chances of a yuan devaluation lingered in the medium-term, depending on China's trade performance. \par \par \uc2  "The possibility of a devaluation of the yuan will increase as the deceleration in Chinese exports becomes more apparent in the months ahead," Kwan said. \par \par \uc2  China's trade surplus in the first 10 months of the year reached $35.59 billion, sharply up from $11.3 billion in the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil in Southeast Asia is likely to hit China's exports next year as prices of goods from East Asian trade rivals, such as Thailand, Indonesia, South Korea, the Philippines and Malaysia, have plunged by up to 50 percent. \par \par \uc2  China's export momentum would be increasingly difficult to sustain as demand from the United States, Europe and Japan, was likely to moderate soon, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Strong export growth was indispensable to China as it needs to keep its economy growing fast enough to provide jobs for millions likely to be thrown out of work as authorities close loss-making state-owned companies, they said. \par \par \uc2  China's official People's Daily on Sunday quoted Vice Premier Zhu Rongji as saying devaluation was not needed to spur exports, but the analysts said Chinese leaders should be well aware of the gains a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation could bring. \par \par \uc2  China gained substantially in competitiveness immediately after the yuan's 33 percent devaluation in January 1994 to 8.7 yuan to the dollar. Exports grew 22.2 percent in 1994 and 21.4 percent in 1995. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtc301i5j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
