\par\par \b \uc2  China  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  rules seen aimed at slowing yuan rise. \b0\par\par\uc2 382 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 1997\par 04:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Nov 4 (Reuters) - China on Tuesday announced new rules allowing domestic firms to retain \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised through stock issues in a move aimed at slowing the appreciation of its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The new rules also allow overseas investors to open accounts in China to retain \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for trading hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  class B shares, the China Securities newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  With the weakness in Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  eating away at China's export competitiveness, Beijing wants to slow the inexorable climb of the yuan, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "China doesn't want companies to pour all the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market, otherwise the yuan will keep appreciating," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended up at a 29-month closing high against the U.S. dollar in Shanghai on Monday -- supported by China's large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, healthy trade surplus and continued inflows of foreign investment, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2810 to one dollar on Monday, the strongest close since May 17, 1995, when it ended at 8.2805. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the yuan has caused the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and recently sparked rumours Beijing would devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan opened down against the U.S. dollar in Shanghai on Tuesday at 8.2830, against Monday's close of 8.2810, due to the new rules but the overall impact was slight since the guidelines were an extension of existing regulations, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the new rules were also aimed at reviving China's flagging B markets by making it easier for foreigners to trade shares and increasing liquidity. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly it points to an attempt to increase or sustain liquidity in the B share market," said an analyst at a foreign securities firm. \par \par \uc2  B shares on China's two stock markets in Shanghai and Shenzhen firmed on the announcement, as well as rises in regional markets, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai's B index closed the morning up 0.08 percent to 65.031 points, while Shenzhen gained 1.49 percent to 115.26 points. \par \par \uc2  -- Shanghai Newsroom (86) 21-6279-7004; Fax 21-6279-7174 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtb4037pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
