\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends at new 28-month high vs dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Sept 24 (Reuter) - The Chinese yuan closed at another 28-month high against the dollar on Wednesday amid expectations that the nation's strong fundamentals will push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  further, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended at 8.2850 to the dollar on Wednesday from Tuesday's 8.2858. It was the highest close since May 17, 1995, when the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished at 8.2805 and was the third consecutive day of 28-month highs. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan continued to rise on Wednesday due to strong market sentiment," a trader said. "Most bank traders believe the yuan will rise in the near term." \par \par \uc2  Trading was active with most business done at about 8.2852, traders said. \par \par \uc2  One dealer said the yuan was likely to rise due to the strong trade surplus coupled with a steady inward flow of foreign investment capital. \par \par \uc2  "Technical charts indicated no signs of correction after Wednesday's close despite past three days of rises," he said. \par \par \uc2  But a second dealer said the yuan might face \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to cap its rise. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yuan from rising sharply. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dt9o022sa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
