\par\par \b \uc2  China's yuan seen under fresh upward pressure. \b0\par\par\uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1997\par 12:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Sept 22 (Reuter) - The Chinese yuan is facing renewed upward pressure against the U.S. dollar because of an oversupply of dollars and increased difficulty of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan is under enormous pressure to appreciate because supply of dollars far exceeds demand," an economist of the China Centre for Economic Research at Beijing University told a seminar at the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "The possibility for the yuan to appreciate in the short term is big," said the economist, who declined to be identified. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at a 28-month high against the dollar in Shanghai on Monday due to widespread market expectations that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had further room to appreciate, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2860 to the dollar compared with 8.2865 on Friday, the highest close since May 17, 1995 when it ended at 8.2805. \par \par \uc2  China has posted a relatively large surplus on both its current and capital accounts from 1994 to 1996, leading to a rising inflow of dollars and also swelling its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the economist said. \par \par \uc2  China's current-account surplus in 1996 stood at $7.242 billion and the capital-account surplus was $39.967 billion, official figures show. \par \par \uc2  China's posted a trade surplus of $25.48 billion in the first eight months and actual foreign investment totalled $26.014 billion in the first seven months. \par \par \uc2  To try to hold down the yuan, the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has resorted to huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through heavy buying of dollars to protect exporters, the Beijing University economist said. \par \par \uc2  But it must recall loans from commercial banks to offset the release of more local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control overall money supply and keep inflation under control, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Further room for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hedge has become smaller and smaller," he said, adding that it was becoming increasingly difficult for the bank to buy dollars without fuelling money supply. \par \par \uc2  The proportion of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans to commercial banks has declined in comparison to its total assets as its purchases of dollars have sent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves soaring, he said. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have hit $131.6 billion, Vice Premier Zhu Rongji said on Monday, up from $105 billion at the start of the year and $21.2 billion at the end of 1993. \par \par \uc2  Loans extended to commercial banks accounted for 52 percent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's total assets in the first quarter of 1997, down from 75 percent in 1993, the economist said. \par \par \uc2  In addition, loans to policy banks, including the Agricultural Development Bank of China and the State Development Bank, are almost impossible to recall, he said. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan should only be allowed to appreciate gradually in the future," said Yang Fan, an economist with the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences, without giving further details. \par \par \uc2  The yuan could rise long-term because it is undervalued against the dollar at current levels despite \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness in neighbouring Southeast Asian nations, Goldman, Sachs and Co said last week. \par \par \uc2  The investment bank forecast the yuan would appreciate to 8.2 against the dollar in 1998 and to 8.0 in the year 2000. \par \par \uc2  -- Beijing Newsroom (86) 10-6532-1921; Fax (86) 10-6532-4978 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: beijing.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dt9m02784\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
