\par\par \b \uc2  China  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  seen stable in first half of 1997. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Graham Earnshaw \par \uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 1997\par 11:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Jan 29 (Reuter) - The yuan is expected to remain stable during the first half of this year, taking a break from its sharp rise over recent months, traders said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The yuan will move in a tight range between 8.2800 and 8.3500 to the dollar with Beijing determined to cap its rise despite steady upward pressure, they said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at a 20-month high of 8.2935 to the dollar on Wednesday on the Shanghai-based national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  interbank market, largely on the back of China's record high \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $105 billion at the end of 1996. \par \par \uc2  "We expect little movement in the yuan against the dollar in the first six months of this year," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a major Chinese bank. \par \par \uc2  "The economy is good with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hitting record highs, but this is being balanced by the higher cost of exports," he added. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not allow the yuan to fluctuate sharply under these conditions." \par \par \uc2  The yuan has risen slowly but inexorably over the past year from around 8.3174 at the beginning of 1996 -- a rise of just 0.29 percent, making it one of the most stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The rise reflects the overall improvement of the Chinese economy, but has added a slight extra burden to exporters. \par \par \uc2  With the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's continued strength, the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on almost a daily basis in the market to check the yuan's upward trend, traders said. \par \par \uc2  One Shanghai foreign trade official said the average export cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood at 8.38 yuan for each dollar of exported goods in 1996, making exporters lose money when they turned trade surplus dollars into local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also bought much local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the interbank market in the past few months, partly to prevent over-supply of the yuan from fanning inflation, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Overall, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is buying in both dollars and yuan to dampen the market, they added. \par \par \uc2  China's retail price inflation fell to a year-on-year 6.1 percent in 1995 from 14.8 percent year-on-year in 1995. \par \par \uc2  But fixed-asset investment surged again in the second half of last year after Beijing cut bank interest rates in May and August, threatening to rekindle inflation, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Despite the fall in inflation last year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to continue to take a cautious approach," said a second \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. "Its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market to prevent sharp rises and falls of the yuan will continue. \par \par \uc2  "The central government has made it clear it will continue its tight credit policy to prevent a revival of inflation," he added. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dt1t02i15\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
