\par\par \b \uc2  China: PBoC To Keep Yuan Stable For Exports - Report \b0\par\par\uc2 181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 1997\par 07:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING (AP-Dow Jones)--The yuan will remain stable against foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite mounting pressure to appreciate, an official report quoted a senior banking official as saying on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  In order to achieve economic targets for 1997, the People's Bank of China will be forced to prevent the yuan from appreciating, the Business Weekly quoted Tao Liming, deputy director of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's International Finance Research Institute, as saying. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will mean that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, now more than $100 billion (U.S.), will expand further, he said. \par \par \uc2  Like last year, the PBoC plans to keep the yuan in a tight range to prevent the yuan rising in value, thereby making the country's exports more expensive. \par \par \uc2  Yet pressure on the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to persist. As the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains a moderately tight monetary policy, the gap between demand and supply of yuan will widen, Tao said. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 19-01-97 \par \par \uc2  0620GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt1j008gc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
