\par\par \b \uc2  China  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  says to keep yuan stable in '97. \b0\par\par\uc2 252 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Jan 19 (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan from appreciating further in 1997 and sees foreign reserves swelling as a result, the China Daily Business Weekly said on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  The yuan would remain stable this year despite mounting pressure to appreciate, the newspaper quoted Tao Liming, deputy director of the International Finance Research Institute under the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of China, as saying. \par \par \uc2  He cited several factors that would keep pressure on the yuan to strengthen this year. \par \par \uc2  The gap between supply and demand of the yuan would persist because of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision to retain moderately tight monetary policy in 1997, he said. \par \par \uc2  Although credit may ease slightly in 1997, the rise in investment would soak up all additional capital, he said. \par \par \uc2  He cited robust growth in foreign investment as another factor. Foreign direct investment, which exceeded $40 billion last year, would indirectly expand the supply of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by providing the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to finance imports, he said, but did not elaborate. \par \par \uc2  In addition, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not let the yuan appreciate to prevent an impact on exports, Tao said. \par \par \uc2  He forecast China's gross domestic product would reach 7,500 billion yuan ($903 billion) in 1997, with about $160 billion coming from foreign trade -- nearly one-sixth of the total. \par \par \uc2  He gave no other details. \par \par \uc2  ($1=8.3 yuan) \par \par \uc2  -- Beijing newsroom (8610) 65321921 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt1j03mtc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
