\par\par \b \uc2  Yuan to fall on expected lower trade surplus. \b0\par\par\uc2 489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par 09:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, March 8 (Reuter) - The Chinese yuan is likely to fall gradually against the U.S. dollar in the next two months due to an expected drop in China's trade surplus in 1996, with 8.50 being a likely resistance level, traders said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has risen steadily over the past year, but traders said they expected a correction, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to maintain exchange rate stability. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan is expected to fall because exports will plunge and imports rise this year on the back of changes in the tax structure," said a trader with the Agricultural Bank of China. \par \par \uc2  Beijing cut tax rebates awarded to firms engaged in export trade twice last year and the current average rate is 8.29 percent from the former 14 percent, which is likely to affect export volume. \par \par \uc2  The China Daily Business Weekly last month quoted Ma Jixian, an official with the State Economic and Trade Commission, as saying export growth was expected to slow and the trade surplus to dwindle to around $6 billion in 1996 from $16.69 billion in 1995. \par \par \uc2  "Demand for the yuan will shrink, while more U.S. dollars are needed with the growth in imports outpacing exports," said an official with the Bank of Communications. \par \par \uc2  Imports were forecast to climb to $158-165 billion in 1996 from $132 billion in 1995, Ma Jixian said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the yuan was likely to fall to around 8.5, a drop of around 3.5 percent from current levels, in spite of new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations that required foreign-funded firms to exchange excess foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for yuan. \par \par \uc2  Foreign banks in selected centres around China that are now allowed to buy yuan off the foreign-funded firms are expected to sell much of the money for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Foreign banks would prefer to hold U.S. dollars instead of renminbi as the dollar is harder in the international market," said a trader from a foreign bank's Shanghai branch. \par \par \uc2  "We are inclined to hold just a little renminbi for routine business and exchange the rest for U.S. dollars," said the department manager of a German bank in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  But traders said the yuan was unlikely to fall sharply as the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep it within a stable range, and would probably defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the 8.5 level, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Both the up and down potential will be limited due to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said an official with the Industrial and Commercial Bank of China. \par \par \uc2  "The 8.5 level will be a resistance point in the next month," said Yang Bin, an analyst with the Shanghai Pudong Development Bank. \par \par \uc2  The closing price of the yuan on the Shanghai-based interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday was 8.3246, a new nine-month low. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds3800hle\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
