\par\par \b \uc2  Taiwan economy seen hit by China missile tests. \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Peng \par \uc2 602 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par 13:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TAIPEI, March 5 (Reuter) - China's planned missile tests in the sea near Taiwan could slash this island's trade, prompt Taiwan dollar selling and drive share prices into a further slump, economists said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  China announced missile tests in the sea around Taiwan from March 8 to 15, apparently as part of military exercises intended to intimidate the island before presidential elections on March 23. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the tests would bring down a partial blockade on the island's two main ports, Keelung in the north and Kaohsiung in the south, through which Taiwan handled 69.9 percent of its trade in 1995. \par \par \uc2  "China's missile tests will likely fully blockade Keelung and Kaohsiung harbours, hitting our imports and exports at least for a short period of time," said Wu Hui-lin, research fellow at Chunghua Institute for Economic Research. \par \par \uc2  "Although importers and exporters can change their shipping routes, or advance or postpone their schedules, the impact is still there, and their moves will raise costs for them, causing inflation several weeks later," Wu said. \par \par \uc2  Wu added that although trading companies may try to switch their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Keelung and Kaohsiung to ports such as Taichung on the west coast and Hualien on the east, he doubted whether such ports had the capacity to handle an immediate flood of new traffic. \par \par \uc2  One of the sea zones in which China will conduct the tests is between 30 and 50 nautical miles west of the island's southeastern coast, near Kaohsiung. The other is between 20 and 40 nautical miles off northeast Taiwan, near Keelung. \par \par \uc2  Thomas Chien, managing director of Wardley James Capel in Taipei, saw China's move as a serious threat. "Of course it would affect Taiwan's economy. It's a serious provocation," Chien said. \par \par \uc2  Chien estimated the missile tests will cause Taiwan's imports and exports in March to drop by some 20 percent. \par \par \uc2  Overseas buyers of Taiwanese goods may postpone purchases, worried that the missile tests will cause disruptions in delivery, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Other economists saw a short-term impact. \par \par \uc2  "It will not be a full blockade, otherwise it would be declaring war. So it should be a short-term strike," said Daniel Chen, chief economist at Chinatrust Commercial Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The biggest impact will be on the stock market and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but since investors and the government has prepared well for this, the actual impact may be limited," Chen said. \par \par \uc2  The government has promoted a T$200 billion (US$7.3 billion) fund to help stabilise a stock market badly hit by tensions with China, Taiwan's second largest export market. \par \par \uc2  Since February 23 to March 2, the fund had bought shares totalling T$12.2 billion (US$445 million), officials said. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not give up defending the Taiwan dollar at T$27.50 against the U.S. dollar, the Economic Daily News on Tuesday quoted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sheu Yuan-dong as saying. \par \par \uc2  The paper quoted Sheu as saying that Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves would be sufficient even if they were to fall to between US$45-50 billion from a level of US$89.6 billion at end-January. \par \par \uc2  Before tensions with China started escalating in June 1995 the reserves were at a record-high of US$100.412 billion, but have fallen as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Chien said that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's threats may cause foreign investors in Taiwan to reconsider long-term investments on the island. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds3500psg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
