\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. Bank/China Yuan -3: Seen Depreciating 2.2% Next Year \b0\par\par\uc2 293 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 1995\par 12:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  To maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band in the face of an excess \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been forced to repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the interbank market, tripling its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to U.S.$70.0 billion since the end of 1993, Morgan Guaranty said. \par \par \uc2  Starting next year, Morgan Guaranty said the mainland's trade surplus is likely to shrink from U.S.$17.1 billion in all of 1995 to U.S.$4.8 billion next year. Export growth should continue to trend downward as exporters face further tax rebate cuts in early 1996. \par \par \uc2  While foreign direct investment this year may match last year's level of U.S.$33.7 billion, 'euphoria has faded as the economy's vigor has cooled,' reflecting foreign investors' 'increasing frustration in dealing with China's bureaucracies.' \par \par \uc2  An anticipated further easing of credit will also put downward pressure on the yuan, despite the prospect of a continued tight monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Morgan Guaranty said China's bid to enter the World Trade Organization (WTO) will mean the country will bring forward the schedule for a freely convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 1997 from the year 2000. That, in turn, means the mainland will have to abolish its current restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions, weakening the government's control over the exchange market and its ability to support the yuan when its fundamentals deteriorate. \par \par \uc2  The yuan should depreciate by about 2.2% against the U.S. dollar next year, with the downward trend continuing in 1997, with the government not expected to resist 'such gradual depreciation, given the competitive pressures already building on exporters,' Morgan Guaranty said. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-11-95 \par \par \uc2  1146GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drbe0930v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
