\par\par \b \uc2  Chinese yuan ends up vs U.S. dollar in Shanghai. \b0\par\par\uc2 222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, May 16 (Reuter) - The Chinese yuan ended higher at 8.2785 against the U.S. dollar, from Monday's 8.2816, but dealers said the yuan would slow its uptrend because appointed banks were seen buying dollars today. \par \par \uc2  The yuan opened at 8.2798 on the Shanghai-based interbank market, the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System, and touched an intra-day high of 8.2705 before dollar buying by the banks trimmed gains. \par \par \uc2  It has hit record highs every day in the past three weeks on a ready supply of the dollars, but dealers said there was no dollar selling in the second half of Tuesday's session. \par \par \uc2  "It seems the dollar is going to reach its bottom price shortly," said one dealer. \par \par \uc2  Floor sources said the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  little. "During the last half hour before the close, dollar buying orders were all from appointed banks and their customers, not from the the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," one source said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan rose to 1.0687 against the Hong Kong dollar, from 1.0706 on Monday. It closed at a record 9.4540 against 100 Japanese yen, from 9.5965, as the yen began sliding on the world market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr5g07sdp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
