\par\par \b \uc2  Chinese yuan ends up against U.S. dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, May 15 (Reuter) - The Chinese yuan ended at 8.2816 to the U.S. dollar on the Shanghai-based interbank market, up from last Friday's 8.2960. \par \par \uc2  The yuan opened at 8.2852 and moved up to touch an intra-day high of 8.2591 before falling back. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has hit record highs every day for the past three weeks. Dealers said there was a ready supply of dollars in the market and the People's Bank of China was buying fewer dollars, making the yuan-dollar rate more market-driven. \par \par \uc2  "As inflation continues to be high, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  less and less in the market," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Experts criticised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fuelling inflation after it bought dollars heavily in the previous few months to boost \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan's rise hurt \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-oriented firms. The government was expected to loosen its controls on import quotas in the second half of this year to promote demand for dollars, the dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan ended higher at 1.0706 against the Hong Kong dollar from 1.0711 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  It ended at a record high of 9.5965 from 9.6925 vs 100 Japanese yen as the yen began sliding on the world market. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr5f07qxc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
